# Rainbow Keys — Requirements

## 1. Keyboard Input
Parent requirement covering all key-to-note behavior.

### 1.1 Octave 1 Mapping
The keys A, S, D, F, G map to the five notes of a pentatonic scale in the first octave, in ascending pitch order (A = lowest, G = highest of that octave).

### 1.2 Octave 2 Mapping
The keys J, K, L, and ; (semicolon) map to the same five-note pentatonic scale one octave higher than Octave 1, in ascending pitch order (J = lowest, ; = highest).

### 1.3 Simultaneous Note Limit
Up to 3 notes may sound at the same time. If a 4th key is pressed while 3 notes are already sounding, the 4th key press does not produce a new note until one of the currently-sounding notes is released.

### 1.4 Note Duration
A note begins sounding when its key is pressed (key-down) and stops sounding when that same key is released (key-up). Notes do not persist after key-up, and do not require repeated key presses to continue while held.

## 2. Visual Feedback
Parent requirement covering all on-screen key representation.

### 2.1 Key Buttons
Each of the 10 playable keys (A, S, D, F, G, J, K, L, ;, and one more to total 10 — see note below) is represented on screen as a distinct button-like visual element, arranged left to right in the same order as the physical keyboard layout.

### 2.2 Active Key Highlight
When a key is pressed and its note is sounding, that key's on-screen element changes to a visibly lighter shade of its assigned color. When the key is released, the element returns to its normal (non-lit) shade.

### 2.3 Color Assignment
The 10 playable keys are assigned colors following the ROYGBIV sequence (Red, Orange, Yellow, Green, Blue, Indigo, Violet), repeating or extending the sequence as needed to cover all 10 keys, assigned left to right in physical keyboard order.

---

## Appendix: Resolved Ambiguity — Open Item

**Status:** Resolved for implementation purposes; flagged here as an open item in case the original intent differs.

The original document above states there are 10 playable keys (2.1) but only enumerates 9 across the two key groups: Octave 1 (1.1) names A, S, D, F, G — 5 keys. Octave 2 (1.2) names J, K, L, ; — only 4 keys, one short of a second full 5-note pentatonic octave.

**Resolution chosen:** Octave 2 is extended with the Quote key (`'`), the physical key immediately to the right of Semicolon (`;`) on a standard QWERTY keyboard. This gives Octave 2 five keys — J, K, L, ;, ' — in ascending pitch order, mirroring Octave 1's five keys (A, S, D, F, G), for 10 playable keys total. This was chosen over alternatives (e.g. reusing a key from Octave 1's row, or a non-adjacent key) because it is the minimal, position-consistent extension of the exact row `J K L ;` the requirements already specify, preserving left-to-right / low-to-high physical ordering per 1.2, 2.1, and 2.3.

**Why this is still flagged as open:** requirements 1.1 and 1.2 as numbered do not mention Quote anywhere; if the original intent was a different 10th key (or a different Octave 1/2 split entirely), those two requirements should be amended and this appendix removed. Implementation (`python/key_mapping.py`) references this appendix directly in a comment at the point where the 10th key is introduced, so the discrepancy stays traceable from code back to this document.
import Combine
import SwiftUI

@main
struct RainbowKeysApp: App {
    @StateObject private var coordinator = AppCoordinator()

    var body: some Scene {
        WindowGroup("Rainbow Keys") {
            KeyboardViewRepresentable(coordinator: coordinator)
                .frame(minWidth: 700, minHeight: 220)
                .onAppear {
                    // Launched as a bare SPM executable (no .app bundle), the process
                    // is not activated by macOS, so its window never becomes key and
                    // keyboard events go elsewhere. Claim regular-app status and
                    // activate explicitly so key capture works immediately.
                    NSApp.setActivationPolicy(.regular)
                    NSApp.activate(ignoringOtherApps: true)
                    coordinator.start()
                }
                .onDisappear { coordinator.stop() }
        }
    }
}

/// Wires UI key events to the Swift/Python boundary and relays the resulting state back to the
/// UI. Owns no note, color, or polyphony logic itself -- all of that is decided by the Python
/// logic layer; this class only routes messages between `KeyboardView` and `PythonBridge`.
final class AppCoordinator: ObservableObject {
    private let bridge = PythonBridge()
    private weak var keyboardView: KeyboardView?

    func attach(_ view: KeyboardView) {
        keyboardView = view
        view.onKeyDown = { [weak self] key in self?.bridge.sendKeyDown(key) }
        view.onKeyUp = { [weak self] key in self?.bridge.sendKeyUp(key) }
    }

    func start() {
        bridge.onColors = { [weak self] keys, colors in
            DispatchQueue.main.async {
                self?.keyboardView?.applyColors(keys: keys, colors: colors)
            }
        }
        bridge.onNoteState = { [weak self] key, active in
            DispatchQueue.main.async {
                self?.keyboardView?.setActive(active, forKey: key)
            }
        }
        bridge.start()
    }

    func stop() {
        bridge.stop()
    }
}
import AppKit
import SwiftUI

/// A single playable key's on-screen element (Req 2.1). Purely a renderer: it draws whatever
/// base color and active/inactive state it is told, and owns no note, pitch, or color-assignment
/// logic of its own -- that all lives in the Python logic layer.
final class KeyButtonView: NSView {
    let keyIdentifier: String

    private let label = NSTextField(labelWithString: "")
    private var baseColor: NSColor = .darkGray
    private var isActive: Bool = false

    init(keyIdentifier: String) {
        self.keyIdentifier = keyIdentifier
        super.init(frame: .zero)

        wantsLayer = true
        layer?.cornerRadius = 10
        layer?.backgroundColor = baseColor.cgColor

        label.stringValue = KeyButtonView.displayText(for: keyIdentifier)
        label.alignment = .center
        label.font = .systemFont(ofSize: 16, weight: .semibold)
        label.textColor = .white
        label.isBezeled = false
        label.drawsBackground = false
        label.isEditable = false
        label.isSelectable = false
        addSubview(label)

        translatesAutoresizingMaskIntoConstraints = false
        label.translatesAutoresizingMaskIntoConstraints = false
        NSLayoutConstraint.activate([
            widthAnchor.constraint(equalToConstant: 56),
            heightAnchor.constraint(equalToConstant: 140),
            label.centerXAnchor.constraint(equalTo: centerXAnchor),
            label.centerYAnchor.constraint(equalTo: centerYAnchor),
        ])
    }

    required init?(coder: NSCoder) {
        fatalError("init(coder:) is not supported")
    }

    private static func displayText(for keyIdentifier: String) -> String {
        switch keyIdentifier {
        case "semicolon": return ";"
        case "quote": return "'"
        default: return keyIdentifier.uppercased()
        }
    }

    /// Applies the base color assigned by the Python logic layer (Req 2.3).
    func setBaseColor(hex: String) {
        guard let color = NSColor(rainbowKeysHex: hex) else { return }
        baseColor = color
        refreshBackground()
    }

    /// Reflects whether this key's note is currently sounding (Req 2.2). The engine (Python)
    /// decides *when* a key is active; this view only decides *how* active looks.
    func setActive(_ active: Bool) {
        isActive = active
        refreshBackground()
    }

    private func refreshBackground() {
        let color = isActive ? baseColor.rainbowKeysLightened(by: 0.35) : baseColor
        layer?.backgroundColor = color.cgColor
    }
}

/// Container for all 10 playable keys (Req 2.1): lays them out left to right in physical
/// keyboard order and captures raw key-down/key-up events (Req 1.4) to forward unmodified.
/// Contains no note-selection, pitch, color-assignment, or polyphony logic -- see
/// `python/key_mapping.py` and `python/audio_logic.py` for that.
final class KeyboardView: NSView {
    /// Physical keys this instrument responds to, left to right (Req 2.1). This is keyboard
    /// layout identity only -- no pitch or color data lives here; that comes from Python.
    static let keyOrder: [String] = [
        "a", "s", "d", "f", "g",
        "j", "k", "l", "semicolon", "quote",
    ]

    /// macOS virtual key codes (Carbon HIToolbox kVK_ANSI_* values) for the keys above. Using
    /// virtual key codes rather than characters makes key capture independent of the active
    /// input source / modifier state, i.e. it tracks physical key position as required by 1.1/1.2.
    private static let keyCodeToIdentifier: [UInt16: String] = [
        0x00: "a", 0x01: "s", 0x02: "d", 0x03: "f", 0x05: "g",
        0x26: "j", 0x28: "k", 0x25: "l", 0x29: "semicolon", 0x27: "quote",
    ]

    var onKeyDown: ((String) -> Void)?
    var onKeyUp: ((String) -> Void)?

    private var buttons: [String: KeyButtonView] = [:]

    /// Keys currently physically held, tracked so they can be released if the window loses key
    /// status mid-press (macOS delivers no keyUp after focus moves away; without this, notes
    /// would sound forever). Raw event bookkeeping only -- no note logic.
    private var heldKeys: Set<String> = []
    private var resignKeyObserver: NSObjectProtocol?

    override init(frame frameRect: NSRect) {
        super.init(frame: frameRect)

        let stack = NSStackView()
        stack.orientation = .horizontal
        stack.spacing = 8
        stack.translatesAutoresizingMaskIntoConstraints = false

        for key in KeyboardView.keyOrder {
            let button = KeyButtonView(keyIdentifier: key)
            buttons[key] = button
            stack.addArrangedSubview(button)
        }

        addSubview(stack)
        NSLayoutConstraint.activate([
            stack.centerXAnchor.constraint(equalTo: centerXAnchor),
            stack.centerYAnchor.constraint(equalTo: centerYAnchor),
        ])
    }

    required init?(coder: NSCoder) {
        fatalError("init(coder:) is not supported")
    }

    override var acceptsFirstResponder: Bool { true }

    override func viewDidMoveToWindow() {
        super.viewDidMoveToWindow()
        window?.makeFirstResponder(self)

        if let observer = resignKeyObserver {
            NotificationCenter.default.removeObserver(observer)
            resignKeyObserver = nil
        }
        if let window {
            resignKeyObserver = NotificationCenter.default.addObserver(
                forName: NSWindow.didResignKeyNotification, object: window, queue: .main
            ) { [weak self] _ in
                self?.releaseAllHeldKeys()
            }
        }
    }

    /// Emits a key-up for every key still held when keyboard focus is lost, since macOS will not
    /// deliver their real keyUp events to this window anymore.
    private func releaseAllHeldKeys() {
        for key in heldKeys {
            onKeyUp?(key)
        }
        heldKeys.removeAll()
    }

    /// Applies the color assignment decided by the Python logic layer (Req 2.3). This view does
    /// not compute colors itself -- it only renders the mapping it is given.
    func applyColors(keys: [String], colors: [String]) {
        for (key, colorHex) in zip(keys, colors) {
            buttons[key]?.setBaseColor(hex: colorHex)
        }
    }

    /// Reflects note-sounding state decided by the Python logic layer (Req 1.3/1.4/2.2).
    func setActive(_ active: Bool, forKey key: String) {
        buttons[key]?.setActive(active)
    }

    // MARK: - Raw key capture (Req 1.4) -- forwarded verbatim; no note/pitch logic here.

    override func keyDown(with event: NSEvent) {
        guard !event.isARepeat, let key = KeyboardView.keyCodeToIdentifier[event.keyCode] else {
            super.keyDown(with: event)
            return
        }
        heldKeys.insert(key)
        onKeyDown?(key)
    }

    override func keyUp(with event: NSEvent) {
        guard let key = KeyboardView.keyCodeToIdentifier[event.keyCode] else {
            super.keyUp(with: event)
            return
        }
        heldKeys.remove(key)
        onKeyUp?(key)
    }
}

/// Hosts the AppKit `KeyboardView` inside the SwiftUI view hierarchy.
struct KeyboardViewRepresentable: NSViewRepresentable {
    @ObservedObject var coordinator: AppCoordinator

    func makeNSView(context: Context) -> KeyboardView {
        let view = KeyboardView(frame: .zero)
        coordinator.attach(view)
        return view
    }

    func updateNSView(_ nsView: KeyboardView, context: Context) {}
}

// MARK: - Color rendering helpers (Req 2.2's "lighter shade" is a rendering detail; the base
// color itself still comes entirely from the Python logic layer via Req 2.3).

private extension NSColor {
    convenience init?(rainbowKeysHex hex: String) {
        var hexString = hex.trimmingCharacters(in: .whitespacesAndNewlines)
        if hexString.hasPrefix("#") { hexString.removeFirst() }
        guard hexString.count == 6, let value = UInt32(hexString, radix: 16) else { return nil }
        let r = CGFloat((value >> 16) & 0xFF) / 255.0
        let g = CGFloat((value >> 8) & 0xFF) / 255.0
        let b = CGFloat(value & 0xFF) / 255.0
        self.init(srgbRed: r, green: g, blue: b, alpha: 1.0)
    }

    /// Returns a visibly lighter shade of this color, used to render the "active" state of a key
    /// (Req 2.2). Blends each channel toward white rather than raising HSB brightness, because
    /// fully-saturated primaries (red, green, blue, yellow...) are already at maximum brightness
    /// and would otherwise barely change.
    func rainbowKeysLightened(by amount: CGFloat) -> NSColor {
        guard let converted = usingColorSpace(.sRGB) else { return self }
        return NSColor(
            srgbRed: converted.redComponent + (1.0 - converted.redComponent) * amount,
            green: converted.greenComponent + (1.0 - converted.greenComponent) * amount,
            blue: converted.blueComponent + (1.0 - converted.blueComponent) * amount,
            alpha: converted.alphaComponent
        )
    }
}
import Foundation

/// Thin process/IPC bridge to the Python logic layer. Contains no note, color, or polyphony
/// logic of its own -- it only launches the Python process, ships raw key events to it as
/// newline-delimited JSON over stdin, and decodes newline-delimited JSON state messages from its
/// stdout back into plain callbacks.
///
/// Boundary choice: a persistent Python subprocess communicating over stdin/stdout pipes, rather
/// than a Unix domain socket or an embedded interpreter (e.g. PythonKit). See the project's
/// implementation summary for the latency/reliability comparison behind this choice.
final class PythonBridge {
    var onColors: (([String], [String]) -> Void)?
    var onNoteState: ((String, Bool) -> Void)?

    private var process: Process?
    private var stdinHandle: FileHandle?
    private var stdoutHandle: FileHandle?
    private var stdoutBuffer = Data()

    func start() {
        let process = Process()
        process.executableURL = URL(fileURLWithPath: "/usr/bin/env")
        process.arguments = ["python3", PythonBridge.bridgeScriptPath]

        let stdinPipe = Pipe()
        let stdoutPipe = Pipe()
        process.standardInput = stdinPipe
        process.standardOutput = stdoutPipe
        process.standardError = FileHandle.standardError

        stdoutPipe.fileHandleForReading.readabilityHandler = { [weak self] handle in
            let data = handle.availableData
            guard !data.isEmpty else { return }
            self?.consume(data)
        }

        do {
            try process.run()
            self.process = process
            self.stdinHandle = stdinPipe.fileHandleForWriting
            self.stdoutHandle = stdoutPipe.fileHandleForReading
        } catch {
            FileHandle.standardError.write(
                "PythonBridge: failed to launch Python logic process: \(error)\n".data(using: .utf8)!
            )
        }
    }

    func stop() {
        stdoutHandle?.readabilityHandler = nil
        stdinHandle?.closeFile()
        process?.terminate()
        process = nil
        stdinHandle = nil
        stdoutHandle = nil
    }

    func sendKeyDown(_ key: String) {
        send(["type": "key_down", "key": key])
    }

    func sendKeyUp(_ key: String) {
        send(["type": "key_up", "key": key])
    }

    private func send(_ message: [String: Any]) {
        guard let handle = stdinHandle,
              let data = try? JSONSerialization.data(withJSONObject: message) else { return }
        handle.write(data)
        handle.write("\n".data(using: .utf8)!)
    }

    private func consume(_ data: Data) {
        stdoutBuffer.append(data)
        while let newlineRange = stdoutBuffer.range(of: Data([0x0A])) {
            let lineData = stdoutBuffer.subdata(in: stdoutBuffer.startIndex..<newlineRange.lowerBound)
            stdoutBuffer.removeSubrange(stdoutBuffer.startIndex..<newlineRange.upperBound)
            handleLine(lineData)
        }
    }

    private func handleLine(_ lineData: Data) {
        guard !lineData.isEmpty,
              let json = try? JSONSerialization.jsonObject(with: lineData) as? [String: Any],
              let type = json["type"] as? String else { return }

        switch type {
        case "colors":
            guard let keys = json["keys"] as? [String], let colors = json["colors"] as? [String] else { return }
            onColors?(keys, colors)
        case "note_state":
            guard let key = json["key"] as? String, let active = json["active"] as? Bool else { return }
            onNoteState?(key, active)
        default:
            break
        }
    }

    /// Resolves the Python bridge entry point relative to this source file's location. This
    /// assumes a development/source checkout layout (running via `swift run`); a distributed
    /// .app build would instead bundle the `python/` directory as a resource and resolve it via
    /// `Bundle.main` instead.
    private static var bridgeScriptPath: String {
        let sourceFile = URL(fileURLWithPath: #filePath)
        let packageRoot = sourceFile
            .deletingLastPathComponent()  // PythonBridge.swift -> Sources/RainbowKeys/
            .deletingLastPathComponent()  // Sources/RainbowKeys/ -> Sources/
            .deletingLastPathComponent()  // Sources/ -> package root
        return packageRoot.appendingPathComponent("python/bridge_server.py").path
    }
}
